# The schedule, in Hong Kong time (the container sets TZ=Asia/Hong_Kong, so a
# line here means what it reads as).
#
# WHY NOT A RACE CALENDAR. Racing is roughly Wednesday evening at Happy Valley
# and Saturday or Sunday afternoon at Sha Tin, but that moves for typhoons,
# public holidays and international meetings — several times a season, and
# silently. Encoding it here would put the calendar in two places and let them
# drift. So the schedule is dumb and the job is smart: `nightly` reads the
# database, works out what is outstanding, and asks HKJC only about the dates
# it cannot already answer. A quiet night is a handful of requests and no
# writes; it does not even run the derive pass.
#
# Five runs a day, each one covering a different way the data goes stale:
#
#   07:00  dividends and vet records published overnight, and tomorrow's card
#   13:00  a card published during the day, before an evening meeting
#   19:00  after a Sha Tin afternoon meeting
#   22:45  after the early Happy Valley races, so the page is current while
#          the meeting is still on
#   23:45  after the last race anywhere
#
# --db is passed explicitly even though HKRD_DB is set. If the environment were
# ever lost, the scrape would write to ./hkrd.db inside the container, every
# count would look correct, and the dashboard would read a different file that
# never changed. That failure is worth guarding twice.

0  7  * * *   python -m hkrd.jobs.nightly --db /data/hkrd.db
0  13 * * *   python -m hkrd.jobs.nightly --db /data/hkrd.db
0  19 * * *   python -m hkrd.jobs.nightly --db /data/hkrd.db
45 22 * * *   python -m hkrd.jobs.nightly --db /data/hkrd.db
45 23 * * *   python -m hkrd.jobs.nightly --db /data/hkrd.db

# The pre-race speed map. Its own line rather than a step inside `nightly`,
# for the reason the trials line gives: a projection that fails must not take
# the race-day scrape down with it, and the two have nothing to say to each
# other.
#
# No date here either. `--pending` asks the database which stored cards have no
# results yet, so the calendar stays in one place.
#
# Three runs, placed against how the card actually changes. It is published and
# revised in the morning, and then SCRATCHINGS arrive right up to the off --
# `field_size` scales both axes of every projection, so a map built before a
# scratching and read after it is wrong for every runner in the race, not just
# the one that came out. The last two lines are that guard: one before a Sha Tin
# afternoon, one before a Happy Valley evening.
20 7  * * *   python -m hkrd.jobs.project_card --pending --db /data/hkrd.db
20 12 * * *   python -m hkrd.jobs.project_card --pending --db /data/hkrd.db
20 18 * * *   python -m hkrd.jobs.project_card --pending --db /data/hkrd.db

# Barrier trials. A separate line because they are a different source on a
# different schedule, and folding them into `nightly` would mean a failed
# race-day scrape takes the trials down with it.
#
# No date and no weekday pattern. Measured over the 2025-26 archive, trials
# fall on Tue, Thu and Fri equally (26.4% of 159 days each), Mon 16.4%, and
# Sat and Wed a handful of times — "Tuesday and Thursday" would miss 47% of
# them silently. HKJC publishes the list of trial days on the page itself, so
# the job reads it and fetches what the database is missing. Trial results
# appear late morning; noon and evening cover a late publication.
0  12 * * *   python -m hkrd.jobs.scrape_trials --db /data/hkrd.db
0  20 * * *   python -m hkrd.jobs.scrape_trials --db /data/hkrd.db

# Live odds. A different cadence again, because odds are the only signal here
# that moves minute to minute — the favourite changes between morning and post
# time in 44% of races, and every odds-dependent figure on the dashboard is
# required to read the LATEST snapshot rather than the morning's.
#
# EVERY MINUTE, and the job decides whether that minute is worth a capture.
#
# The schedule is the dumbest line in this file on purpose, for the same
# reason `nightly` has no race calendar in it: the interesting question is
# "how far is this race from its off", and the database is the only thing that
# knows. `scrape_odds.CADENCE_MINUTES` holds the answer — hourly the day
# before, every 15 minutes from three hours out, every 5 from thirty minutes,
# and EVERY MINUTE through the last ten and past the off. That last band is
# the point: the late money does not show until the final five to ten minutes,
# and a flat 15-minute schedule samples the only window that moves three times.
#
# A tick with nothing due costs 0.3s and no network. Simulated against a real
# Sha Tin card, 1,103 of 1,501 ticks over a meeting's 25-hour cycle answer
# from the database alone; the other 398 make two requests.
#
# --quiet, because an idle tick that announced itself would put ~1,400 lines of
# "nothing to price" a day into `fly logs` and bury the runs that captured
# something. It silences ONLY the idle tick: a zero with something attempted,
# and every skip, still speak.
#
# No hour restriction either, and that is deliberate rather than lazy. HKJC
# opens a market at 13:00 the day BEFORE racing, so a 12:00-23:59 window would
# capture Saturday evening, stop at midnight, and resume at noon on Sunday —
# thirteen hours of silence ending thirty minutes before race 1. The job looks
# at today's meeting first and falls through to tomorrow's, so the overnight
# market is recorded rather than lost.
#
# ARMED. It was commented out for as long as the capture needed a browser: the
# image carries no Chromium, so the line would have failed every quarter hour —
# loud, useless noise rather than a working capture — and the odds table stayed
# at whatever the legacy import rescued. `ingest/odds.py` now reads the JSON
# endpoint the betting site's own front end reads, which needs nothing the
# image does not already have, so a meeting costs one request and under a
# second.
#
# SIZE. Measured, not estimated: ~63,000 rows and ~6.7 MB per meeting, so
# ~0.6 GB a season, against a 1 GB volume that nothing ever deletes from.
# Extend the volume before the second season — see docs/deploy.md.
* * * * *   python -m hkrd.jobs.scrape_odds --db /data/hkrd.db --quiet
